  "course_description": "An introductory course on the nature and methods of history. May be repeated for credit when topic varies.",
  "course_notes": "(Same as AFS 270 2) (Complete topic: Law, Markets, and Empires in the Atlantic World) (For centuries, the Atlantic world has been a theater of contestation-from early maritime ventures and the four-century-long slave trade to relatively recent scrambles for territorial control. Too often, however, economic histories have privileged European perspectives, reducing markets to abstract spaces governed by supply and demand, and historical actors to mere profit-maximizers. By centering Africans and their descendants, this course challenges such static assumptions and asks: how did plural legal regimes, spatial politics, and different definitions of value shape the Atlantic world? Focusing on the seventeenth to nineteenth centuries, it will explore the entwinement of law, markets, and empires through disputes over currencies, commodities, customs, and commerce across four continents. In so doing, this course traces how African litigants, merchants, and rulers actively negotiated trans-Atlantic exchange, while illuminating why struggles over property, wealth, and sovereignty still resonate today.)",
